      "subject": "Re: Integrating byLLM into our product - partnership?",
      "message": "Hi Chris,\n\nThanks for reaching out. It's great to hear that byLLM outperformed your existing pipeline in both accuracy and cost during your prototype phase.\n\nI am the right point of contact to discuss this further. I'd be happy to explore a deeper integration and discuss possibilities for a partnership, including co-marketing or a case study.\n\nI have availability for a call over the next two weeks. Please let me know a few dates and times that work for you, or feel free to send over a calendar link.\n\nBest,\n\n[Your Name]"
    },
    {
      "to": "fhassan@uwaterloo.ca",
      "subject": "Prospective GSoC contributor - where to start?",
      "message": "Hello Fatima,\n\nThank you for your interest in contributing to Jaseci! It's great to hear that you're already familiar with Python and have been exploring the Jac language.\n\nTo get started, I recommend exploring our main GitHub repositories. A great way to dive in is by looking for issues labeled \"good first issue,\" which are specifically curated for new contributors to help them get acclimated to the codebase.\n\nWe look forward to seeing your contributions. Please feel free to reach out if you have any further questions!\n\nBest regards,"
    },
    {
      "to": "omar.said@nexusanalytica.com",
      "subject": "Bug: edge traversal returns duplicates after node delete (repro attached)",
      "message": "Hi Omar,\n\nThanks for bringing this to our attention. This isn't currently a known issue, so please do file a GitHub issue with the full snippet so we can investigate it immediately.\n\nAs a temporary workaround for production, you might try manually clearing the adjacency lists of the neighboring nodes immediately following the deletion.\n\nBest,\n\n[Your Name]"
    },
    {
      "to": "grace.liu@techledger.news",
      "subject": "Re: Press: quote on agentic programming languages (deadline Aug 5 EOD)",
      "message": "Hi Grace,\n\nHere is a quote for your piece:\n\n\"General-purpose languages are designed for linear execution and static data structures, which struggle to handle the dynamic, autonomous, and asynchronous nature of agentic software. Agent-native languages provide the necessary primitives for decentralized coordination and adaptive reasoning that traditional languages simply weren't built to support.\"\n\nHope this helps! Let me know if you need anything else before your deadline.\n\nBest,\n\n[Your Name]\nJaseci"
    },
    {
